COMPRESS-PDF UNDER `PROCESSING_PIPELINE=on`: THE PROBE'S DOWNLOAD, AND THE PRODUCT'S
Audit host, 2026-09-04. Artifact BUILD_ID VWgZdjW1LEZvaUo6nwJJi, built at f6f0fa8.
Origin https://172.20.10.2:3051 (TLS front) -> 127.0.0.1:3052. Same artifact for both
runs below; the ONLY difference is one environment variable.

Why this file exists. The upload-boundary closeout reran the tool matrix twice. On the
shipped default it reproduced the documented row. With `PROCESSING_PIPELINE=on` — a flag
no deployment file sets — the compress-pdf row failed. A failing row is not allowed to be
waved away, so it was measured rather than argued.

── the two runs, verbatim ──────────────────────────────────────────────
$ node scripts/tool-runtime-matrix-probe.mjs --url … --only compress-pdf     # PIPELINE=on
  [FAIL] compress-pdf        the page announced a result but pressing Download produced no file
  TOOL RUNTIME MATRIX — PASS 0/1 exercised      PRODUCT FAILURE 1

$ node scripts/tool-runtime-matrix-probe.mjs --url … --only compress-pdf     # flag absent
  [PASS] compress-pdf        8218 bytes, starts "%PDF", named multipage-fixture-compressed.pdf (via gs)
  TOOL RUNTIME MATRIX — PASS 1/1 exercised

The full-matrix figures either side of the same line: 29/29 exercised, 0 product failures
on the default; 28/29 with one PRODUCT FAILURE with the flag on.

── the mechanism, read before it was measured ─────────────────────────
The probe presses the product's own Download control and reads the Blob the handler
passed to `downloadBlob`: it overrides `URL.createObjectURL` and
`HTMLAnchorElement.prototype.click`, and only records a click whose href is a
blob URL it minted (scripts/tool-runtime-matrix-probe.mjs:178-208).

The pipeline path does not produce a Blob, deliberately:

  hooks/useProcessingJob.ts:263     window.location.assign(`/api/jobs/${jobId}/result`);
  components/tools/runners/PipelineToolRunner.tsx:25-27
    "Download is a redirect. The result is fetched by navigating to
     /api/jobs/:id/result, which 302s to a short-lived signed URL, so the output
     never passes through this component's memory."

A navigation is not an anchor click, and no Blob is ever minted, so
`window.__probeDownloads` stays empty however well the product works. The probe is
measuring a delivery mechanism this path does not use.

── so the delivery was measured at the layer that owns it ─────────────
Anonymous, cookie jar carried so job ownership resolves, fixture
docs/qa/p1/multipage-fixture.pdf (4888 bytes):

$ POST /api/jobs?slug=compress-pdf                     HTTP 202  status queued
$ GET  /api/jobs/<id>   (poll)                          running processing
                                                        completed done  outputBytes 8219
                                                        outputFileName multipage-fixture-compressed.pdf
                                                        resultAvailable=True
$ GET  /api/jobs/<id>/result        (the exact URL the browser navigates to)
  HTTP 302
  cache-control: no-store
  content-disposition: attachment; filename="multipage-fixture-compressed.pdf"
  location: https://172.20.10.2:3051/api/storage/file?key=<key>&verb=get&expires=…&sig=<redacted>
$ GET  <that signed URL>                                HTTP 200  8219 bytes
  cache-control: private, max-age=0, no-store
  first bytes: %PDF-1.5

The pipeline path serves the same output the default path serves, through a signed
short-lived URL, with an attachment disposition and no-store — which is what the panel's
comment says it does.

── classification ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NOT EXERCISED, not a pass and not a product failure: the compress-pdf Download row of
the tool matrix cannot observe the `PROCESSING_PIPELINE=on` path, because that path
delivers by navigation and the probe only sees Blobs. P3, harness-side. The remedy is a
probe that also records `window.location.assign` / `Page.navigatedWithinDocument` for a
download URL; it is NOT a product change, and product code is frozen at f6f0fa8 for this
closeout, so nothing was edited to make a row go green.

Two smaller facts, recorded so neither reads as a regression later:
  · gs output is 8218 or 8219 bytes run to run on the same input — Ghostscript writes a
    varying byte, so a one-byte difference between two rows is not a fidelity change.
  · `PROCESSING_PIPELINE=on` is not the shipped default and no deployment file sets it,
    so the row that governs launch is the passing one.

No cookie, token or signature value appears above; the signed URL's `sig` and `key` are
redacted, and the session/anon cookie was held in a curl jar and never printed.
